  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Kentucky has $8.2B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. The state recorded 80,348 awards with an average award size of $102K. Obligations fell 21.2% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $7.5B. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest industry by obligations at $5.5B. HUMANA GOVERNMENT BUSINESS INC is the largest vendor by obligations at $5.5B. Recent fiscal years show a decline from FY23 and FY24 where obligations were $12.5B and $12.3B respectively, before falling to $10.4B in FY25 and to $8.2B in FY26. Other federal buyers in Kentucky include the Department of the Treasury at $183M, the Department of Veterans Affairs at $156M, and the Department of Justice at $154M. Industries beyond health insurance with meaningful obligations include All Other Support Services at $568M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $370M. The concentration in defense buying and health insurance shapes who wins contracts and which sectors see the most federal dollars this fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal spending in Kentucky?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $7.5B. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers received $5.5B. The top two buyers and the top industry together account for the majority of FY2026 to date obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What industries and vendors dominate contracts in Kentucky?",
        "body": "Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest industry at $5.5B. HUMANA GOVERNMENT BUSINESS INC is the largest vendor at $5.5B. Other notable industries include All Other Support Services at $568M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $370M, and other top vendors include LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $563M and THALLE - BAUER LLC at $345M."
      }
    ]
  },
